## Serve the application in several languages

Three steps: put every page under its language, translate the interface files, translate the stored content. Each step works without a translation engine — texts are then copied as they are and marked `pending`, and the first run with a real engine replaces them without being forced.

### 1. Put every page url under its language

```yaml
# config/packages/wexample_symfony_translations.yaml
wexample_symfony_translations:
    locale_routing: true
```

Every route becomes `/{_locale}/…`, whichever bundle declared it, and `/` redirects to the home page of the visitor's language. Paths reached by a program keep their url: anything opening on `/_`, `/api`, `/js/routing`, `favicon.ico` and `robots.txt`. They are answered in the language last chosen on a page — a `_locale` cookie — or else in the one the browser asks for. The list is `locale_routing.excluded_paths`, a set of regular expressions.

The languages are `framework.enabled_locales`, the default locale first. An url typed without its language is redirected to the same page in the visitor's. Adding one rebuilds the cached routes on its own: no cache to clear.

Every page carries its language and its writing direction on `<html>` — `lang="ar" dir="rtl"`, `lang="en-GB" dir="ltr"` — whichever layout drew it. The direction comes from the ICU locale data, not from a list kept by the bundle; `locale_direction('ar')` gives it in Twig, and each entry of `locales()` carries its own.

Two things in the application may still assume urls without a language:

- `access_control` patterns in `security.yaml`: `^/login/2fa` becomes `'^/[a-z]{2}/login/2fa'` — quoted, as `{2}` would otherwise be read as YAML.
- Urls written by hand in javascript. Routes generated through the loader's `RoutingService` take the page's language on their own.

### 2. Translate the interface

```bash
php bin/console translations:translate-files fr
php bin/console translations:translate-files fr es de    # several locales, one after the other
```

Writes a `.fr.yml` next to every `.en.yml` of the application, then adds `fr` to `framework.enabled_locales` in `config/packages/translation.yaml`. Only the application's own files: the bundles' belong to their packages. `--include-bundles` writes into them as well, which is what a developer working on the bundles through symlinks wants.

A key is translated when the target file lacks it, or when its source changed since. A wording already in the target file without a trace in the `.translations.lock.json` beside it was written by hand, and is never overwritten. References (`@page::title`), the `%` wildcard and `~extends` are copied as they are; parameters (`%name%`, `{count}`) and markup never reach the engine. A target file the lock knows whose source file is gone is removed with its lock entry; one the lock does not know was not written by the command, and stays.

| Option | |
|---|---|
| `--dry-run` | Report what would be written, write nothing |
| `--path=<text>` | Only the source files whose path contains it |
| `--from=<locale>` | Translate from another locale than the default one |
| `--force` | Translate again what an engine translated; hand-written wording is kept |
| `--keep-orphans` | Keep the translated files whose source file is gone |
| `--include-bundles` | Also write into the bundles' translation directories |

A key missing in a language is read in the fallback one, so a page translated in part shows the rest in the default language rather than raw keys.

### 3. Translate the content

Mark the fields in the entities — see [translatable entities](translatable-entities) — then translate what is already stored:

```bash
php bin/console translations:translate-content                                  # every content locale
php bin/console translations:translate-content fr
php bin/console translations:translate-content fr --entity='App\Entity\Article'
```

Without it nothing is lost: a field is translated the first time it is read in a language, and stored. The command only spares the first readers the wait.

Content may be read in more languages than the interface has — through the api, or `translated(article, 'it')` — as it costs no file, only rows made on demand. Those are listed apart:

```yaml
wexample_symfony_translations:
    content_locales: [it, nl, pl, tr, vi]
```

The content locales are the interface ones plus these. An api request is answered in the one its `Accept-Language` asks for among them; its interface wording then falls back to the default language. Nothing is ever sent to the engine for a language outside the list, whatever a request asks for.

### Let the reader switch

`wexample/symfony-translations-ds` ships the switcher, a menu of every enabled language, each leading to the same page in it:

```twig
{{ component(render_pass, '@WexampleSymfonyTranslationsDsBundle/components/locale-switcher') }}
```

Without it, `locales()` returns the same list — `code`, `name` as the language names itself, `url` of the current page in it, `current`.

### Plug a translation engine

Every text goes through `Wexample\SymfonyTranslations\Interface\TextTranslatorInterface`, which receives a batch and returns it under the same keys. The default implementation, `PendingTextTranslator`, returns the texts untouched. An engine is plugged by aliasing the interface to its own service:

```yaml
services:
    Wexample\SymfonyTranslations\Interface\TextTranslatorInterface:
        alias: App\Translation\MyTextTranslator
```

Its `getEngineName()` is stored next to each translation; what the pending one produced is redone on the next run of either command, and on the next read of a field.

### Translate with Syrtis

```yaml
# config/packages/wexample_symfony_translations.yaml
wexample_symfony_translations:
    syrtis:
        api_key: '%env(SYRTIS_API_KEY)%'
        session_secure_id: ses_...       # a session on the translation scenario
        host: https://api.syrtis.ai      # the default
        max_batch_length: 6000           # characters of texts per request, the default
```

Translations are then made by a Syrtis scenario and recorded under the engine name `syrtis`. Texts go by batch — a whole file, cut when its texts exceed `max_batch_length` characters, as the model answers a batch in one go. Each request sends to the session two messages stamped `translate`:

- `LANG_CONFIG`, JSON: `{"source": "fr", "target": "en_GB", "source_name": "French", "target_name": "English (United Kingdom)"}`. Locales are Symfony's identifiers, as they are; the names come from the ICU data, in English, for the prompt to say to the model. The scenario keeps no table of languages: any locale ICU knows can be asked for.
- a conversation message, JSON: the texts by key, `{"page.title": "Bienvenue [#0]", "7": "Payer"}`.

The scenario answers with a conversation message holding a JSON object of the translations under the same keys, leaving the `[#n]` placeholders untouched. Keys it leaves out are asked once more on their own; those still missing stay untranslated — read in the fallback locale, reported by the command — and are asked again on the next run. A reply that is not a JSON object stops the command.

Several runs may go at once, each for its own locales: the lock is re-read and updated under a file lock for each file, so none erases the others' entries.
